00:21:56.874 --> 00:21:58.498
FRANCESC CAMPOY
FLORES: So, for me, I'm

00:21:58.498 --> 00:22:00.430
going to go with something
completely selfish.

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:03.850
So we launch many, many
products during the year.

00:22:03.850 --> 00:22:06.430
And it is hard to keep up.

00:22:06.430 --> 00:22:09.340
So when we had product
managers coming and explaining

00:22:09.340 --> 00:22:11.920
what their product was,
it is really useful

00:22:11.920 --> 00:22:14.020
for us and for our
audience, but, specifically,

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:16.510
for me because then I
can tell people about it.

00:22:16.510 --> 00:22:19.120
And one of my favorite
episodes on this

00:22:19.120 --> 00:22:21.910
was the one on Cloud Spanner.

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:25.600
To start with, because we had
the PM, Deepti Srivastava.

00:22:25.600 --> 00:22:27.820
And she did an amazing job.

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:29.800
Also, especially,
she was about to have

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:31.860
a baby, which was amazing.

00:22:31.860 --> 00:22:34.000
And we were like, really,
if you don't want to--

00:22:34.000 --> 00:22:34.700
and she was like, yeah, yeah.

00:22:34.700 --> 00:22:35.440
No, I'm going to make it.

00:22:35.440 --> 00:22:36.760
It was like, OK, perfect.

00:22:36.760 --> 00:22:39.070
So she did an amazing job.

00:22:39.070 --> 00:22:41.350
And also, she was
able to tie it back

00:22:41.350 --> 00:22:44.590
to while Spanner was an
internal thing at Google,

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:47.050
and how it went from
a project to something

00:22:47.050 --> 00:22:48.940
Spanner as a
Service, internally,

00:22:48.940 --> 00:22:51.820
and then how we make that into
something public, and Google

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:52.870
Cloud Spanner.

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:55.615
And that perspective
of the project

00:22:55.615 --> 00:22:57.490
that allows you to see
not only the features,

00:22:57.490 --> 00:23:01.120
but why it got to be the way
it is, is something that only

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:03.010
really product managers
or engineers that

00:23:03.010 --> 00:23:05.470
have been working in the project
for a long time can give.

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:07.530
So that was an amazing
episode, for me.

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:07.660
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:23:07.660 --> 00:23:09.150
I thought that episode
was pretty great, too.

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:11.500
And she's so clear in the way
she explains it, especially

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:13.010
when she covers things
like the CAP theorem

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:14.410
and how that's
related to Spanner.

00:23:14.410 --> 00:23:14.900
It was great.

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:16.108
FRANCESC CAMPOY FLORES: Yeah.

00:23:16.108 --> 00:23:17.320
That was really, really good.

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:18.490
MELANIE WARRICK:
Mark, what about you?

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:19.240
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:23:19.240 --> 00:23:21.280
So I'll take a
different tack as well.

00:23:21.280 --> 00:23:24.310
So we, technically,
started this back in 2016,

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:27.760
but we did three or four
episodes on SRE and SRE Culture

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:29.140
